** Combining Offset Pushdown with locking read patch **

We consider the case of SELECT queries with LIMIT(n) /OFFSET(p). As it
stood, the storage engine returned all the rows that satisfied the
WHERE clause, and the SQL layer took care of discarding the p rows
accounting for the OFFSET and selecting up to n rows. In cases where
p>>n, this led to a large number of rows sent from the storage engine to
the SQL-layer only to be discarded by the latter.

**************** Feature 1: OFFSET PUSHDOWN ************************

We developed a feature called offset pushdown in which the OFFSET is
handled in the storage engine, whenever possible. A certain number of
conditions have to be met in order for this optimization to be possible,
but it essentially revolves around whether the WHERE clause can be
handled in its entirety by the storage engine as it is a necessary
condition for the OFFSET to be handled there as well. If the WHERE
clause requires a SQL-layer check to discard some rows, it will not be
possible to push the OFFSET down to the storage engine as we might skip
rows that would otherwise have been discarded by the SQL-layer,
resulting in an incorrect output.

The feature is implemented as follow, a function offset_pushdown
implemented in sql_select.cc is called in sql_optimizer.cc and
assesses whether the query is a candidate for offset pushdown, and
communicates to the storage engine whether to pushdown down the OFFSET.
The list of conditions for offset pushdown to take place is detailed in
the function comments and is given here for information:

There must be an OFFSET
Only one table
It should not be a temporary internal table (this would require some
implementation in Temptable and InnoDB-intrinsic)
The table should be accessed by a simple access method (using only one
index - no index merge, or using a table scan)
The table in the query must be an InnoDB table
No HAVING (sits between table reading and OFFSET applying, and alters
the result, so OFFSET cannot be applied before HAVING)
No aggregation (same, needs all rows)
No GROUP BY (same)
No ROLLUP (same)
No windowing (same)
No filesort in SQL layer (same)
Condition must be empty - thereby making sure that there is nothing to
check up in the SQL layer
No post-processing temporary table (it may shuffle or filter rows)
No SQL_CALC_FOUND_ROWS (the logic for accurate counting of skipped
rows, necessary for this, is in the LimitOffsetIterator, not in the
engine).
LIMIT is not an extreme value (or the substraction we do to adjust the
offset and the limit in offset_pushdown would be wrong)
The storage engine has been modified to be able to act on that
information, it can now skips the first relevant p rows.

In row0sel.cc, row_search_mvcc has been modified so that it can skip rows if necessary
when offset pushdown is activated. The function skip_row_for_offset_pushdown
assesses whether a given row must be skipped. When the OFFSET should be
pushed down, offset_pushdown communicates pushed_offset = p (0 otherwise) and scanned_offset= 0.
The first call to row_search_mvcc is going to skip rows and increment
scanned_offset up to p. The second call to row_search_mvcc is going to
then read the rows to be returned, if any.

The modifications work also when MVCC confirmation is needed, and for locking reads.

**Optimizer switch: offset_pushdown

Defaults to on

**Optimizer hint:

OFFSET_PUSHDOWN() enables offset pushdown

NO_OFFSET_PUSHDOWN() disables offset pushdown

The hint takes precedence on the state of the optimizer_switch.

Note that offset pushdown is deactivated when parallel query can
potentially be used, as there are not compatible.

************** Feature 2: locking read patch **********

This commit is the last one of a series which re-introduces
the Offset Pushdown feature.

The initial offset pushdown patch was lacking proper handling of
locks in case of locking reads (like SELECT FOR UPDATE and
IN SHARE MODE).

a/ Consider a query:
SELECT * FROM table_1
    WHERE col_1 BETWEEN a AND b LIMIT n OFFSET p FOR UPDATE;

When scanning a range (read_range_next()), the range's end is
checked at the handler level.
With offset pushdown (OP), rows are
skipped in row_search_mvcc() without ever going back up to the
handler as it would without OP.
Thus, if the range's size was actually smaller than the value of
OFFSET, with OP we were scanning and locking more rows than
necessary.
This had an impact on next key locking and gap
locks rendering some inserts and updates impossible when using OP.
We fixed that  by implementing a systematic end-of-range
check in InnoDB for locked records skipped using OP.
We decided not to enable this systematic end-of-range check
for non-locking reads as the skipping  stops anyway at the
end of the page if it exceeds the end of the range, which is good
enough.

b/ Additionally, the rows were skipped as soon as we knew we could
which was fine as long as no locks are to be placed. But if read rows
are to be locked, we need to make sure that said lock is in place
before we skip it. As it stood, the first patch skipped the rows
before PK access, and locks on PK records could not be placed, only
the non-PK locks were placed (lock on secondary index).

When trying to update the primary key to an already used value  on a
row that only had a lock on the secondary index, instead of getting
ER_LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT, we would get an error saying that we had a dupli-
cated primary key.

We fix this by waiting until both PK and non-PK locks  are placed,
before skipping the row for locking reads.

This fix had an unintended side effect:

Skipped rows were now locked. Unlocking is normally done by the
handler layer (unlock_row()), for READ COMMITTED (RC) and READ
UNCOMMITTED (RU). But the handler layer never sees rows skipped by
OP. So  added some unlocking for them in InnoDB.

While a/ defintely had to be fixed, b/ was fixed out of an abundance
of caution. It is hard to tell if the missing PK locks could
cause isolation level rules to be broken (eg. phantom reads)

A note unrelated to this patch, for the record: regarding ICP,
testing showed that locks are not released on rows skipped by ICP.
For RC and RU, this means that it will not be possible to update or
delete rows that were skipped by ICP, while it is possible to
update these very same rows if they had been skipped without ICP
(in the SQL layer).

OP does not modify the existing locking logic: In the case of a
query with offset pushdown and ICP, the ICP locking logic is
applied. In the case of a query with offset pushdown without ICP,
the default locking logic is applied.

Note that with rds_empty_redundant_check_in_range_scan = ON
(default), ICP might be deactivated for a given query. In that
case, the locking procedure would revert to the default locking
logic in which locks are released rows not returned to the client
(for RC and RU).

A counter innodb_rds_rows_skipped_by_offset_pushdown was added
so that we could inspect from mtr tests how many rows were skipped using
offset pushdown.

add mtr for offset pushdown

No tmp table written to disk by optimizer

1. Need qep_tab[const_tables]
2. No tmp table written to disk by optimizer
